How to cover a cake with crumbs neatly?

We’ve repeatedly been asked this question in DM, so we decided to make a short video in which we would share the main tips of this simple technique:

- first, grind the dry crumbs in a food processor, wet — using hands, and then pass them through a large sieve so that the crumbs become fine and uniform;

- secondly, in order for crumbs to hold well on the sides of the cake, you should do this technique immediately after the final leveling. If using ganache, work especially quickly;

- thirdly, start applying crumbs from the very bottom, gradually moving up. Scoop up a large handful and gently press it to the surface. Repeat until the desired part of the cake is covered. After that, work with a small spatula — scoop up the crumbs from the cake board and pull it up with a light, careful movements. In this way, you will close the gaps and remove the excess, making the coating as uniform as possible;

- and, fourthly, work on a turntable and do not forget to put a large sheet of parchment paper next to it, so that cleaning the kitchen does not take half a day later.

That’s all! Nothing complicated, and the cake already looks festive.

This type of colorings is used to color foods with a high water content.

- liquid water-soluble clorings are suitable for water-based glazes, marshmallows, zephyr or fruit pastes (pastila);

- gel water-soluble colorings, in which the percentage of moisture is lower than that of liquid ones, can be used to color sponges, low-fat frostings, as well as to create all kinds of patterns on confectionery products by applying the colorings to a pastry bag;

- powder (dry) water-soluble colorings are suitable for coloring products that are highly sensitive to moisture: for example, meringues or macarons.
